<section class="grid h-[1533px] w-full grid-cols-1 gap-8 overflow-hidden px-4 py-16 md:h-[1150px] md:grid-cols-[320px_720px] md:px-8 md:py-32">
  <p class="m-0 font-body text-base leading-5 font-normal text-primary uppercase">
    <span class="hidden md:inline">01 / The feeling of a place</span>
    <span class="inline md:hidden">The feeling of a place</span>
  </p>
  <div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-8 md:w-[720px]">
    <p class="m-0 text-base leading-[27px] text-dark">
      Some places stay with you long after you’ve left. Not because they were
      the grandest, or the newest, or the hardest table to get. You remember
      them because of how they made you feel.
    </p>
    <p class="m-0 text-base leading-[27px] text-dark">
      A chair pulled out before you asked. A menu that felt good in your hands.
      The particular warmth of a room as the evening settled in. Small things,
      perhaps. But together, they become the reason you want to return.
    </p>
    <h2 class="m-0 font-display text-[36px] leading-[1.05] font-normal text-dark uppercase">
      More than what’s
      <br />
      on the plate.
    </h2>
    <p class="m-0 text-base leading-[27px] text-dark">
      We talk a lot about food when we talk about restaurants. And rightly so.
      But a meal never arrives on its own. It arrives in a setting, with a
      voice, carried by people who shape the experience as much as the kitchen
      does.
    </p>
    <p class="m-0 text-base leading-[27px] text-dark">
      Before the first course, a place has already told you something about
      itself. The sign outside. The words on the website. The way you’re
      greeted. Each detail sets an expectation; the best places find a way to
      keep that promise, quietly and consistently.
    </p>
    <p class="m-0 text-base leading-[27px] text-dark">
      That’s where identity becomes more than a logo. It becomes a way of making
      decisions. What belongs here? What feels like us? What would make someone
      feel at home?
    </p>
    <h2 class="m-0 font-display text-[36px] leading-[1.05] font-normal text-dark uppercase">
      The details do
      <br />
      the talking.
    </h2>
    <p class="m-0 text-base leading-[27px] text-dark">
      A good menu doesn’t just list dishes. It gives you a sense of the people
      behind them. A room’s materials, its music, even the space between the
      tables all add to the story. These things don’t need to match perfectly.
      They need to feel like they come from the same place.
    </p>
    <p class="m-0 text-base leading-[27px] text-dark">
      When the details are considered together, the experience feels natural.
      Nothing is working too hard to explain itself. You can relax into it. And
      that ease is often the thing you remember.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="flex h-[348px] flex-col gap-10 overflow-hidden bg-primary px-4 py-16 text-light md:h-[620px] md:p-32">
  <p class="m-0 font-body text-base leading-5 font-normal text-light uppercase">
    A note to come back to
  </p>
  <blockquote class="m-0 font-display text-[38px] leading-[1.05] font-normal uppercase md:text-[72px]">
    “The places we love
    <br />
    don’t just feed us.
    <br />
    They make us feel
    <br />
    we belong.”
  </blockquote>
</section>

<section class="grid h-[1409px] w-full grid-cols-1 gap-8 overflow-hidden px-4 py-16 md:h-[1188px] md:grid-cols-[320px_720px] md:px-8 md:py-32">
  <p class="m-0 font-body text-base leading-5 font-normal text-dark uppercase">
    02 / A reason to return
  </p>
  <div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-8 md:w-[720px]">
    <h2 class="m-0 font-display text-[36px] leading-[1.05] font-normal text-dark uppercase">
      Familiar, without
      <br />
      standing still.
    </h2>
    <p class="m-0 text-base leading-[27px] text-dark-grey">
      There’s a particular pleasure in returning to a place you know. The
      familiar table. The person who remembers your order. The sense that, for a
      little while, you’re part of its rhythm.
    </p>
    <p class="m-0 text-base leading-[27px] text-dark-grey">
      That familiarity isn’t the same as sameness. A place can change its menu,
      refresh its rooms, or grow into something new while keeping hold of its
      character. What matters is knowing what sits at the heart of it — and
      giving that room to evolve.
    </p>
    <p class="m-0 text-base leading-[27px] text-dark-grey">
      For us, that’s the work of hospitality branding: finding what makes a
      place itself, then helping it show up in every part of the experience.
      From the first impression to the last little detail.
    </p>
    <h2 class="m-0 font-display text-[36px] leading-[1.05] font-normal text-dark uppercase">
      Make something
      <br />
      worth returning to.
    </h2>
    <p class="m-0 text-base leading-[27px] text-dark-grey">
      The most memorable places aren’t trying to be everything to everyone. They
      have a point of view. They know who they are, who they’re there for, and
      what they want to put into the world.
    </p>
    <p class="m-0 text-base leading-[27px] text-dark-grey">
      Start there. With the people, the purpose, and the feeling you want
      someone to take away. Then let the details follow. Because a place stays
      with us when it gives us something to carry — and a reason to come back.
    </p>
    <div class="m-0 flex flex-col gap-8 border-t border-light-grey py-8">
      <p class="m-0 text-base leading-[27px] text-dark">
